text:
maria: heads-up: our sample app hit token refresh storms on flaky mobile networks. multiple parallel requests all end up doing refreshSync() and we got 429s from token endpoint. repro on Pixel 7 Android 14 and 15.

devon: ouch. is refreshSync() calling the same OkHttp/Retrofit client?

maria: yes. interceptor does a synchronized(tokenLock) { if (token == null) token = refreshSync(); } but there's a subtle window where many threads see non-null? also we retry on 401 by calling refreshSync() again without coordination. code: 
```kotlin
class TokenAuthInterceptor : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    var token = tokenStore.get()
    if (token == null) {
      synchronized(tokenLock) {
        if (tokenStore.get() == null) {
          token = refreshSync() // uses retrofit with same OkHttpClient
          tokenStore.set(token)
        } else {
          token = tokenStore.get()
        }
      }
    }

    val req = chain.request().newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer $token")
      .build()

    val resp = chain.proceed(req)
    if (resp.code == 401) {
      // naive re-refresh + retry
      synchronized(tokenLock) { tokenStore.set(refreshSync()) }
      return chain.proceed(req.newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer ${tokenStore.get()}").build())
    }
    return resp
  }
}
```

leo: two problems: 1) refreshSync reusing same client -> deadlocks/queue starvation; 2) synchronized check-then-act still allows thundering herd on the retry path because multiple requests can reach 401 and refresh simultaneously if timing aligns.

maria: server returned bursts of 429 then started blacklisting for a minute. user-visible failures.

sdk-bot: quick suggestion: use an in-flight dedupe (singleflight) so only one refresh happens; make refresh calls on a dedicated NoAuth client; avoid blocking main network dispatcher.

devon: we've used kotlin coroutines Mutex + a Deferred<...> holder to share in-flight refresh result. gist: 
```kotlin
suspend fun getToken(): String = coroutineScope {
  inflightRefreshMutex.withLock {
    inflight?.let { return@coroutineScope it.await() }
    val d = async { refreshTokenSuspending() }
    inflight = d
    try { d.await() } finally { inflight = null }
  }
}
// Retrofit call uses a small separate OkHttpClient without interceptors
```

leo: for non-coroutine apps we published an AtomicReference<CompletableFuture> singleflight pattern in SDK docs. Also added sample that exercises network flaps (toggle airplane mode) to catch the race.

maria: implemented coroutine singleflight + separate client and it fixed the repro locally. adding a test that simulates 10 parallel requests while token is expired.

devon: can you add a short note in sample README about token endpoint rate-limits and recommending backoff + jitter on refresh? we should also show how to configure a tiny dispatcher for the refresh client: maxRequests=2, maxRequestsPerHost=1.

maria: will do. opening PR with code sample and test. pinging SDK folks for review.

ci-bot: build/24124 started for maria/token-singleflight-fix. running unit + integration tests.

maria: thanks! assigning follow-ups: add docs entry for "Avoid token refresh storms" and a migration snippet for Java (CompletableFuture singleflight). :coffee:
